using System.Text.Json;
using System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata;

var options = new JsonSerializerOptions(JsonSerializerDefaults.Web)
{
    TypeInfoResolver = new DefaultJsonTypeInfoResolver()
};

// 1) GetTypeInfo<T>() — strongly-typed, no cast
JsonTypeInfo<Person> personInfo = options.GetTypeInfo<Person>();
string json = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new Person("Ada", 36), personInfo);

// 2) TryGetTypeInfo<T>() — check without throwing
if (options.TryGetTypeInfo<Person>(out JsonTypeInfo<Person>? info)) { /* use info */ }

// Returns false (not throws) when metadata is unavailable:
var empty = new JsonSerializerOptions { TypeInfoResolver = new NullResolver() };
empty.TryGetTypeInfo<UnknownType>(out _); // → false
```

**Key takeaways:**
- `GetTypeInfo<T>()` returns `JsonTypeInfo<T>` directly — no casting from the non-generic overload.
- `TryGetTypeInfo<T>(out JsonTypeInfo<T>?)` returns `false` when the resolver can't produce metadata, avoiding try/catch.
